Isotopic composition, atomic mass, and a material’s molar mass (@gc-isotopes: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @general-chemistry, #source "../sources/Atomic-Weights-and-Isotopes.lingua#r_1HF97SGP3GF6YJDRH0D81PQ3S3", #source "../sources/IUPAC-Green-Book.lingua#r_5SR8CP04E580VSV69MYQNSZ73F", #general-chemistry "../../../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/foundations/Units-and-dimensions.lingua#r_3A511KGNX59K2YHKT83NM38NE1") { r_7RS9YA931BVZ7RVM0KSWJ2W7Y3
**Read:** [Atomic weights, isotopes, and the periodic table — CIAAW and IUPAC](../sources/Atomic-Weights-and-Isotopes.lingua#r_1HF97SGP3GF6YJDRH0D81PQ3S3), table definitions, notes, and the selected element entries; [Quantities, Units and Symbols in Physical Chemistry — IUPAC Green Book](../sources/IUPAC-Green-Book.lingua#r_5SR8CP04E580VSV69MYQNSZ73F), amount, masses, and chemical composition quantities.

An atomic-weight value may summarize a particular isotope composition rather than a universal exact constant for every specimen. Read CIAAW's interval and uncertainty notes before adding digits from a convenient periodic table. Distinguish a relative isotopic mass, a natural-material standard atomic-weight interval, an abridged teaching value, and a measured sample-specific composition.

For an idealized two-isotope teaching mixture with relative masses 10 and 11 and amount fractions 0.75 and 0.25, the mean relative mass is 10.25. These are invented exact values for learning weighted averages, not a claim about an actual element's isotope masses or abundances. Changing the isotope fractions changes the mean without changing atomic number.

Molecular and formula molar masses combine the relevant elemental values with stoichiometric counts. Hydration, isotope enrichment, composition variation, and purity can matter to an actual mass-to-amount conversion. Keep the source and chosen precision explicit. The post-2019 mole definition fixes the Avogadro constant; it does not make every measured molar mass exact.

**Work:** derive the weighted average and its dependence on one independent isotope fraction. Compute a formula molar mass from an identified table and compare a rounded classroom value with the source's stated precision. Explain why an isotope mass number in square brackets on a periodic table should not be silently treated as the standard atomic weight of an ordinary sample.

Compare CIAAW's current table with the particular IUPAC downloadable table edition. Record differing update dates. Follow [Units, dimensions, and equations that can make sense](../../../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/foundations/Units-and-dimensions.lingua#r_3A511KGNX59K2YHKT83NM38NE1) for the shared treatment of quantities, numerical values, dimensions, and conversions before using amount-of-substance calculations.

